description:
Create deterministic, reusable fault fixtures and an integration protocol test that exercise the full f-phase order-management stack: intents and FSM (f.1), coordinator (f.2), submitter (f.3), and fill processor (f.4).

tests/fixtures/order_events.py:
(1) Provide deterministic builders for normalized broker order updates and fills with stable event ids, client_order_ids, UTC-aware timestamps, and Decimal quantities and prices.
(2) Provide a scriptable in-memory fake Broker port that can be programmed to: accept; reject; time out on submit while having actually accepted; time out with the order absent; fail lookups; emit duplicate events; emit events out of order; emit partial fills followed by cancel; emit a protective-stop fill racing an application exit; and simulate a crash between intent persistence and the broker response.
(3) Use a fixed injected clock and no randomness; any seeds must be fixed constants. Use an isolated temporary SQLite database or in-memory store per test.

tests/integration/test_order_lifecycle.py contains end-to-end protocol scenarios run through the public APIs only:
(a) happy path: INTENT_PERSISTED -> SUBMITTING -> ACCEPTED -> PARTIALLY_FILLED -> FILLED, with the held qty and cost basis correct;
(b) ambiguous submit where the order was actually accepted: a lookup by client_order_id finds it, there is exactly one submit, and no fill is inferred;
(c) ambiguous submit with lookup failure: the order stays UNKNOWN, entries for the symbol are blocked, and there is no resubmit or new client_order_id;
(d) duplicate and out-of-order events produce the same final ledger as the in-order stream;
(e) partial fill then cancel: the remainder reservation is released and the cycle holds the partial qty;
(f) cancel requested then fill arrives: the fill is applied and the order ends FILLED, not CANCELED;
(g) a protective stop fill races an EXIT_LONG: the coordinator refuses the oversell, or the fill processor records drift, and the held quantity never goes below zero;
(h) crash after a persisted intent and before the response: recover_inflight resolves it by lookup and does not auto-resubmit;
(i) concurrent entries on one account cannot double-reserve cash;
(j) SHADOW and PAPER account keys remain fully isolated.

The integration test must also assert that replaying the same fixture stream twice yields byte-identical persisted order/ledger projections, as a determinism check.

Before editing, inspect: mike_trade/execution/order_fsm.py, mike_trade/execution/intents.py, mike_trade/execution/coordinator.py, mike_trade/execution/submission.py, mike_trade/execution/fill_processor.py, mike_trade/brokers/ports.py, mike_trade/persistence/transaction.py, mike_trade/persistence/repositories.py, and the existing tests/unit/test_order_fsm.py, tests/unit/test_order_serialization.py, tests/unit/test_ambiguous_submit.py, and tests/unit/test_fill_processor.py, so you reuse their conventions. If a production defect is found, do not fix it here. Mark the scenario with pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True) and a clear reason that names the defect, so the failure stays visible.
